          "via": "straight",
          "line": "solid",
          "arrow": "end"
        }
      ],
      "caption": "A chain of biases: the doing/being distinction gets blurred, three habits of mind exploit that blur, and language plus hype push the illusion further, ending in a warning that noticing the illusion isn't enough."
    },
    {
      "id": "inside-arguments",
      "title": "Inside: Four Arguments Against Computation",
      "height": 460,
      "items": [
        {
          "kind": "node",
          "id": "split",
          "shape": "box",
          "x": 20,
          "y": 60,
          "w": 170,
          "h": 70,
          "text": "No software/hardware split in brains",
          "size": "xs",
          "detail": "Unlike a computer, a brain's biology and its function can't be neatly separated into hardware and software.",
          "block": "spya-b0086e"
        },
        {
          "kind": "node",
          "id": "replace",
          "shape": "box",
          "x": 20,
          "y": 180,
          "w": 170,
          "h": 70,
          "text": "Silicon neuron swap fails",
          "size": "xs",
          "detail": "You can't perfectly replace one biological neuron with a silicon one - real neurons do things like clearing metabolic waste.",
          "block": "spya-ahtr6e"
        },
        {
          "kind": "node",
          "id": "beyond",
          "shape": "box",
          "x": 210,
          "y": 60,
          "w": 170,
          "h": 70,
          "text": "Beyond step-by-step algorithms",
          "size": "xs",
          "detail": "Brains are full of continuous and random physical processes that strict algorithms cannot represent.",
          "block": "spya-n393ru"
        },
        {
          "kind": "node",
          "id": "governor",
          "shape": "box",
          "x": 210,
          "y": 180,
          "w": 170,
          "h": 70,
          "text": "A steam governor isn't a computer",
          "size": "xs",
          "detail": "Some systems just do physics directly - calling them computation is a category error, and brains may be similar.",
          "block": "spya-sq9v5k"
        },
        {
          "kind": "node",
          "id": "predict",
          "shape": "box",
          "x": 400,
          "y": 60,
          "w": 170,
          "h": 70,
          "text": "Perception is the brain's best guess",
          "size": "xs",
          "detail": "What we consciously see and feel is a running prediction about the world's hidden causes, not raw data.",
          "block": "spya-dp8sem"
        },
        {
          "kind": "node",
          "id": "body",
          "shape": "box",
          "x": 400,
          "y": 180,
          "w": 170,
          "h": 70,
          "text": "Predictions exist to keep the body alive",
          "size": "xs",
          "detail": "Regulating heart rate, oxygen and other vital signs may be exactly why felt experience exists at all.",
          "block": "spya-b59nm2"
        },
        {
          "kind": "node",
          "id": "wet",
          "shape": "box",
          "x": 584,
          "y": 60,
          "w": 152,
          "h": 70,
          "text": "A simulated storm isn't wet",
          "size": "xs",
          "detail": "A computer model of a process isn't the same as the process itself actually happening.",
          "block": "spya-npjt4j"
        },
        {
          "kind": "node",
          "id": "bostrom",
          "shape": "box",
          "x": 584,
          "y": 180,
          "w": 152,
          "h": 70,
          "text": "Assumes AI consciousness to work",
          "size": "xs",
          "detail": "The famous argument that we probably live in a simulation only works if running the right program creates real experience.",
          "block": "spya-xdy4fe"
        },
        {
          "kind": "node",
          "id": "together",
          "shape": "ellipse",
          "x": 190,
          "y": 320,
          "w": 380,
          "h": 90,
          "text": "Together: computational functionalism looks shaky",
          "size": "md",
          "detail": "No single argument is fully decisive alone, but together they weaken the case that computation alone creates consciousness.",
          "block": "spya-uzr60x"
        },
        {
